WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.380
- One, two, three, four, five.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:02.700
Hello, hello, hello.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.660
Okay, here we go.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.670
I wanna explain to you
what a recession is.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.010
I'm doing this because
you're gonna hear this word

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:08.880
a lot lately.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.440
And depending on how bad it gets,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.420
you'll likely feel its effects.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.450
So give me 12 minutes
to explain recession.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.283
Plus, of course,

00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:18.480
the inevitable time it
takes me to read an ad,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.530
which is something I must do

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.110
in order to pay my employees
and feed my children.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.490
I'm sure you can understand.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.040
I spent four years in
university studying economics

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.587
preparing for this very
moment, so let's go.

00:00:28.587 --> 00:00:31.620
- Are we at a turning point
headed for a recession?

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.720
- Storm clouds of recession looming

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.070
and the storm is really coming.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.450
- With inflation at a 40-year high,

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.512
many experts warn of a
possible economic slow down.

00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:41.220
- A recession is almost inevitable.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.900
We use the word "economy"

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.570
to summarize what is
literally billions of things.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.030
Billions of people making
billions of decisions

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.320
all connected to each other,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.510
and yet the result of
all of these decisions

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.400
is one of two things,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.442
this line either going up or going down.

00:00:57.442 --> 00:01:00.090
(ominous music)

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.580
This all-encompassing number on a graph

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.897
is called the gross domestic product, GDP,

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:07.680
which, by the way, is kind
of the most undescriptive,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.140
useless title ever given to anything.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.750
GDP is like the receipt of an economy.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.840
Every single thing that was
made and sold in an economy

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.570
all added up into one giant number.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.310
Now there's a very technical definition

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.780
that you can go explore in the sources

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:22.920
if you're that kind of person,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.680
but for now, just imagine every TV

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.830
and piano lesson and slice
of pizza and car wash

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.930
and pair of shoes and
smartphone and video game,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.790
everything that the people purchased

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.610
from the businesses in
your economy in one year

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.400
all added up and packed into this one dot

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.633
and plopped on a graph.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.060
So what we're looking at here
is Britain's GDP in 1990.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.890
Everything that the economy made and sold

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.950
to the people added up to
about a trillion pounds.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.600
That was their GDP for that year.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.670
This is the best way that we can summarize

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.430
how big an economy is,
how much it produces,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.500
how well it's performing year after year.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.430
Let's look at a graph.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.770
This is what GDP looked
like in the old days,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.060
like in the agrarian days.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.100
You had ups and downs year by year,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.590
but really, overall, this
graph is pretty flat.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.960
The economy didn't really
have an upward trend.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.890
It wasn't growing.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.350
That's because back in the 1300s

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.780
the economy was a
completely different world

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.740
than what we live in today.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.750
It was pegged to things like farming,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.380
which was pegged to things
like weather or plague or war.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.940
So like here's the Black Death

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.370
that killed 40% of Europe's population,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.140
and here is the Great Frost,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.450
a weather event that
ruined a bunch of crops

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.730
and therefore made the
economy shrink for a year.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.040
Okay, so this is how it
was in the agrarian days,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.024
but brace yourself for what happens next.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.230
(upbeat music)

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.050
This flat line just
turned into a steep cliff,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.360
and that's because humans
started doing science,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.100
not just to make themselves smarter,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.350
but because they were incentivized to,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.900
to make more stuff to,
make it more efficiently.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.210
All of this happening as
humans were developing

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.580
and spreading a new set of economic rules,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.220
rules that rewarded those who invented,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.280
produced, and sold stuff
that made life better

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.380
for both the buyer and the seller.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.330
The system was called capitalism.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.790
Capitalism plus industry

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.460
was the cocktail that
allowed this to happen.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.020
By the way, pause the timer for a second

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.880
because I need to just say it is painful

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.370
how little I'm able to
include on this topic.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.040
Go into the sources and just poke around

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.560
and you'll see so much good juicy history

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.720
on capitalism and the
Industrial Revolution.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.310
Let's move on. Back to the timer.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.950
So every year people could
make more and more stuff.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.300
They could sell more stuff.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.280
And there was more and more GDP,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.170
more stuff being made
and sold in the economy,

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.250
not just in England,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.380
which is this graph we've
been using as the example,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.290
but eventually in the whole world.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.600
Okay, but there was a
catch to this new system.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.070
Unlike the old agrarian days

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.120
where you could have a graph

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.400
that kind of stays
generally flat over time,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.320
this new system was built off of the idea

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.530
that the line kind of has to go up.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.200
Capitalism works because
it incentivizes people to

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.660
make products and
businesses that are better

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.760
and better every year,
more and more productive,

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.150
pushing this line higher
and higher every year.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.340
And the kind of voodoo
magic of capitalism is

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.165
if the line doesn't go
up, bad things happen.

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:11.915
(dramatic music)

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.530
Okay, so here's where
it all comes together.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:17.940
We're trying to get to recession right?

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.260
By the mid 1800,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.570
the capitalist experiment
was fully underway,

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.210
at least in the western world and Japan.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.070
The rest of the world was
being brutally exploited

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.270
to make all of this happen.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.740
Anyway, topic of another story.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.840
See How Europe Stole the
World, part one and two,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.371
part three coming soon.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:35.670
(exhales) The amount of
stuff being made and sold

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.550
in the economy was going up
more and more every year.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.790
This graph is definitely going
up, but what about all these?

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.790
Soon a mysterious pattern
emerged in this line.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.700
Every few years the
GDP graph would go down

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:48.570
for a little bit.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.840
The amount of stuff being
made and sold would shrink,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.870
not necessarily tied to
weather and war and plague,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.670
but just cause.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.230
Before eventually recovering

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.090
and continuing on its way up.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:04.410
And these little dips in
the line we call recession,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.710
a receding of the amount of
shit we can make and sell.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:11.160
Here is the GDP of the
United States since 1948

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.020
and here are all the recessions.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.130
There've been like 12.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.420
I mean, we just got out of one of these.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.640
We had a very short but dramatic recession

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.050
thanks to COVID-19.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.480
But the last long recession was back here,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.430
the financial crisis around 2008

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:24.600
the one that you probably think of

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.010
when you hear the word recession.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.510
- Subprime derivative.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.340
- A subprime mortgage meltdown.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.566
- A housing bubble.

00:05:30.566 --> 00:05:31.560
- Okay, wait, wait, wait.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.420
But why does the graph go
down in the first place?

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.890
Like what makes it go down?

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.350
You can't answer that by
looking at just this big line.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.210
You have to zoom into one of these dots

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:42.561
and see what has to happen
to create this number.

00:05:42.561 --> 00:05:45.144
(upbeat music)

00:05:46.054 --> 00:05:48.330
At the very heart of
the capitalist economy

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.680
you have two major players.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.450
You've got people and businesses,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.690
or as economists like to call
it, households and firms.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.770
In this case, let's just say

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.780
the entire economy of your country is

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.220
actually the size of your neighborhood,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:00.840
and you're going to your local bakery,

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.880
an important business in
your neighborhood economy.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.500
You and your neighbors
show up to this bakery

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.450
and you pay $3 for a loaf of bread.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.760
The bakery is a business
that uses that money to

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.110
pay its workers,

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.630
but these workers aren't like
some special class of people.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.430
They're just other people in the economy

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.350
who also are gonna go spend money too.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.110
The bakery also has to
pay rent to a landlord

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.930
who's also just another
person in the neighborhood.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.670
So those people then go off

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.530
with their new money
that they just got paid

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.660
and they spend on other businesses

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.930
who also have workers
to pay and rent to pay.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.480
And suddenly your money is flowing

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.520
to all the people who work in the economy,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.350
and everyone's feeling good about it.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.150
They have jobs, they have a solid income,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:40.740
they can buy cool stuff.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.540
The bakery and other
neighborhood businesses

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.159
feel this demand and they go,

00:06:44.159 --> 00:06:47.040
"we need to hire more people
so we can make more bread."

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.260
And lucky for them, there are investors,

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.420
and the investors are
stoked on all of this demand

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.220
so they start investing in the bakery

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.570
and all the other businesses,

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.250
giving them money to hire more people,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.340
to make more bread,
more buying, more jobs,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.830
more stuff being added to the total.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.343
The GDP is going up,
the economy is growing.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.000
Okay, but what happens when
some outside force comes in

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.130
to disrupt all these good vibes?

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.710
It all starts when you show
up to your bakery one day

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.600
and you notice that something changed.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.150
Suddenly the price of a loaf
of bread is actually $4.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:18.983
And the baker's like,

00:07:18.983 --> 00:07:21.600
"Sorry, dude! The price
of flour has gone up."

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.670
You know, inflation,
supply chains, whatever.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:24.630
They say something.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.280
But the point is what it does to you.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:27.210
Suddenly you're feeling like, oh,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.470
maybe I can't afford this bread.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:31.080
Or even if you can, you
see that something's up

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.180
or maybe you hear that the
interest rate is going up,

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.740
something that we're
hearing about a lot lately.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.970
And so now you're thinking, oh,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.060
maybe I shouldn't spend money
on expensive artisan bread

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.670
but save that money and I can
actually get a good return.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:42.503
If you're an investor,

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.070
the higher interest rate's
kind of freaking you out

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:46.950
because now it's more
expensive to borrow money.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.410
You think twice before
investing in the bakery.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.060
Another option is that
you're hearing rumors

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.400
about a coming economic downturn

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.050
because of some war somewhere,

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.480
and you judge that it would
be wise to save your dollars

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.840
instead of spend on expensive
artisan bread or whatever.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.280
Meanwhile, your neighbors are seeing

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:03.810
and feeling the same things,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:04.920
and they decide that this bread

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.600
is a little bit too expensive for them

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.040
and they'll just make bread at home.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.890
Less bread is demanded
from the bakery, AKA,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.290
there's a disruption in
your local bread market,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.960
AKA, there's a mismatch
between bread demanded

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:16.950
and bread supplied.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.980
The bakery hired too many
workers during the good times.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.570
They now don't have the
revenue to pay them all,

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.270
so they have to lay some of
them off or cut their pay.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.107
Unemployment is now going up.

00:08:26.107 --> 00:08:27.660
"I lost my job today,"

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.300
says your sad looking
neighbor one evening,

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.340
which kind of freaks you out even more.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.620
Maybe you should be more
careful about your spending.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.300
And now it's starting to spread.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.700
What was just a decision
to not buy bread anymore

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.720
means that many employees in
the neighborhood won't get paid

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.400
and now they can't go out and spend money,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.890
which means that other
businesses have less revenue

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.390
to pay their employees,

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.670
and now more people are hearing about this

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.860
and deciding maybe they
don't wanna be spending,

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.560
and suddenly there are fewer
and fewer transactions,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.300
less lending and investment.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.880
And now this economy that was
once buzzing with activity

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.100
is experiencing a domino effect.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.670
Less stuff is being made,
less stuff is being bought.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:08.250
Your neighborhood economy
is literally shrinking.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:09.510
It's making less stuff.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:12.540
Your GDP is lower than
it was the year before.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.973
Your neighborhood is in recession.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.890
The point I wanna make here
is that this is the result

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.600
of loads and loads of people

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.310
making a bunch of micro-decisions

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.140
on whether or not to spend their money.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.370
And because of the system we're in,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:29.130
these decisions have a
massive chain reaction

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.900
that affects everyone who participates

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.300
in the economy, meaning everyone.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.940
That's the blessing and
the curse of capitalism.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.490
Okay, let's just pull
ourselves out of the analogy

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.750
with all the pretty animation

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.550
and talk about the real
world for a second.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.400
You're probably wondering, are
we in a recession right now?

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.020
Are people spending less money

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.910
so that the economy makes less stuff?

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.770
Is GDP doing this?

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:52.140
I mean, maybe!

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:53.430
We don't know yet!

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.080
And especially with this one,
it's kind of a weird one.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.070
It's hard to tell.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.620
We're getting contradictory
signals from the economy.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.520
Here in the United States

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.470
we had two quarters in a row,

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.080
which is like six months,
of a shrinking GDP,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.610
and this, for a lot of analysts,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.110
is the definition of a recession.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.480
Two quarters in a row of
shrinkage? We're in a recession.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.300
But then just a few weeks
ago, the news came out

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:16.290
that the third quarter of
2022, the economy grew again.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.210
I mean it was by less
than 1%, but it grew.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.250
So that's not a recession yet.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.660
Plus, during a recession,

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.210
the number of unemployed people spikes.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.790
But check out where the
unemployment rate is right now.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.530
It's nice and low.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:29.880
That's not recession.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:31.590
So the signals are contradictory.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:32.970
By the way, if you want a deeper dive

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.910
into how recessions are
officially classified and declared

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.070
and by whom, check the
sources in the description.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.200
There's a whole super deep dive into that.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.870
Most of you would be like
snoozing after two seconds

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:43.703
if I went into it.

00:10:43.703 --> 00:10:46.470
What we do know is that people
are a little bit freaked out.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.330
They're being more cautious
about their spending,

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.760
and that's because of a cocktail of causes

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:51.990
that you've heard about in the news.

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.870
COVID-19 giving way to energy prices

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.810
and supply chain disruptions
and this war in Ukraine.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:02.250
The result is less of
this and more of this,

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.110
and that's because hundreds
of millions of people

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:05.790
are making hundreds of millions

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.180
of little micro-decisions
every day to spend less money

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:12.690
in an economy that only works
if everyone spends more money

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.607
and pushes businesses to make more stuff.

00:11:14.607 --> 00:11:16.770
And that's the point I want to make here.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:17.640
The big takeaway.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.670
We talk about the economy like
it's some kind of science.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.280
I mean, I have a degree in
economics, a social science.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.020
We look at graphs, we do calculus,

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.310
and we feel like we're scientists.

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:27.840
And in a big sense we are.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.010
But what this graph hides

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.950
is that what we're dealing
with here are people,

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:34.830
people who make decisions
based on how they feel,

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:37.320
and that's really hard
to predict and control.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.153
We do it.

00:11:38.153 --> 00:11:39.090
See the inflation video

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.490
for how the Fed is like a puppet master.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.100
We do it, but it's really
hard to control and predict.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.680
So a recession is almost certainly coming.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.630
You may be watching
this in like nine months

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:49.650
and the recession is here.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:50.483
I don't know.

00:11:50.483 --> 00:11:53.100
The fact is by looking at
this line, what we can say

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.100
is that a recession is natural and normal.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.320
It is a part of our
modern economic system.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.750
It will likely come and then go.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.780
People will lose jobs,
businesses will close,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.840
and at some point, for a
million tiny little reasons,

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.260
things like policy and
just general feelings

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:14.040
in a bunch of people's guts,
this graph will stop going down

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:15.960
and it will start going up again.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.493
That we can be sure.

